# RF-Core Trace Capture and Decode Pipeline - Architecture

**Status:** As-built (shipped, tests green). No open decisions; the decoder-language
question was settled by measurement (section 12).
**Date:** 2026-07-04
**Scope:** A customer-shippable replacement for the internal capture appliance that decodes
the CC-series RF-core hardware tracer log sink (`source/ti/log/LogSinkTraceLPF3`). Off-the-shelf
logic-analyzer hardware, native decode, Wireshark. Reuses the SDK log-metadata model and the
existing `tilogger` decode and dissector conventions so RF-tracer logs look identical to ITM.

This document is the self-contained ground truth for the pipeline: the wire format, the
component layout, the interfaces, and the rationale. It does not point at any external spec;
the facts a maintainer needs are stated here.

---

## 1. Context

CC-series chips ship a `ti/log` framework with four interchangeable sinks. Three already have
host tooling; the trace sink did not - that gap is what this pipeline closes.

| Sink | Transport | Rate | Host tool |
|---|---|---|---|
| `LogSinkBuf` | on-chip RAM ring | - | debugger / ROV (no stream tool) |
| `LogSinkITM` | Arm CoreSight ITM / SWO pin | 12 MHz typ. | `tilogger itm` (Python) |
| `LogSinkUART` | UART2 peripheral | 3 Mbaud | `tilogger uart` (Python) |
| `LogSinkTraceLPF3` | RF-core tracer, one DIO pin | up to 24 Mbaud | this pipeline (`tracedecode` + `tilogger rftrace`) |

The trace sink is a dedicated tracer block inside the RF core, not the general-purpose UART2.
`LogSinkTraceLPF3` writes log records straight into the tracer's registers; the tracer
serializes them onto a user-selectable DIO pin as a UART-like line. There is no software
buffer and no software timestamp - the tracer hardware stamps each record.

**Two protocol layers, kept separate throughout the code:**

- **Physical line** - a UART-like serial line but not a standard UART: idle high on the real
  wire, active-high start bit, 10 data bits MSB-first, trailing zero. 12 bits per frame.
  Recover words by start-edge detection plus oversampling.
- **Logical packet** - a framed protocol above the word layer: a per-channel state machine
  `SOP -> [timestamp] -> header(dbgid) -> data... -> EOP` with a 5-bit CRC trailer and a
  channel-0 timestamp-MSB sync word. Each 10-bit word is `[9:8] channel-id . [7:0] payload`.
  A `dbgid` (3..255, scoped to a channel) maps to a printf format string via the dbgid database.

Section 3 states both layers in full so no other reference is needed.

## 2. Pipeline architecture

```
[CC chip: LogSinkTraceLPF3 -> RF-core tracer -> one DIO pin]
   physical: 12-bit frame, idle-high, active-high start, MSB-first, NOP=0x000 idle fill
   logical:  8-bit dbgid . arg-count . up to 4 params . 16-bit HW timestamp (0.5 us tick)
        | single trace line
        v
   [ Sampler ]  one of:
     - sigrok-cli --continuous -O binary   (true streaming, endless)
     - Logic 2 Automation API              (batched .sal capture windows)
     - a pre-recorded .sal or raw file     (offline / CI)
        | raw samples (1 byte/sample) or a Saleae .sal container
        v
   [ tracedecode : native decoder ]
        sample ingest -> deframe -> 10-bit words -> classify
          -> per-channel packet state machine (x3) + CRC-5 + timestamp
          -> resolve (channel,dbgid) against the dbgid database -> printf
          -> LogRecord
        | pcap, DLT_USER0=147, ||-delimited text payload (identical to tilogger)
        +-- stdout (human line)
        +-- Wireshark via extcap or a fifo, reusing tilogger_dissector.lua
        +-- dumpcap ring buffer (last-N retention)
        +-- in-memory tail (last-N, no Wireshark)
        v
   [ tilogger rftrace transport ]  (optional front-end)
        spawns tracedecode, reads its pcap back, re-emits tilogger LogPackets
        so every tilogger output works unchanged; --elf resolves dbgids from the ELF
```

The decoder is the only throughput-critical custom component. Sampler, viewer, and retention
are off-the-shelf; metadata resolve and the dissector reuse the existing SDK / `tilogger` design.

## 3. Wire format (ground truth)

### 3.1 Physical layer

- One DIO pin driven by the RF-core tracer. Bit clock is 24 MHz on most parts; some variants
  run it at 48 MHz (use `--divide-time-by-2` so timestamps read as wall-clock).
- **12-bit frame**, MSB drives the pin first:
  - bit 11 (first): start = 1 (active-high, the opposite of a standard UART's active-low start)
  - bits 10:1: the 10-bit data word, MSB first
  - bit 0 (last): 0 (trailing/stop-like)
- Bit clock / 12 gives the frame rate. At 24 MHz that is 2 MHz words = 500 ns/frame = 20 Mbit/s
  of payload. The idle line is a continuous stream of NOP words (`0x000`), which is what lets
  clock recovery lock even when no logs are flowing.
- **As captured by the Saleae the line reads inverted** (idle low). So the deframer treats it
  as inverted async-serial: 24 Mbaud, 10 data bits, MSB-first, 1 start, 1 stop, no parity. The
  "idle-high / active-high start" of the real wire reconciles with this through the inverted probe.
- At 500 MS/s the ratio is 500e6 / 24e6 = 20.83 samples/bit - non-integer. The deframer places
  bit centers with a fractional accumulator and resyncs phase on every start edge, so only
  intra-frame drift (12 bits) matters. Both clocks are crystal-derived, so intra-frame drift is
  far below half a bit; no running period estimate is needed.
- **Polarity is auto-detected, not assumed.** Because the tracer idles with NOP frames (1 start
  bit, 11 zero bits) whenever it is on, the start-bit level is always the *minority* level over a
  window. The deframer picks the minority level as the start level, which makes the "inverted"
  flag only a hint and lets both real captures and synthetic captures decode with one default.

### 3.2 10-bit word layout

Every word is `w[9:8] = channel-id`, `w[7:0] = payload`. Classify by masks:

| Word | Detect on 10-bit `w` | Fields |
|---|---|---|
| NOP / idle | `w == 0x000` | none - dropped |
| Overflow | top bits 0, `w & 0x00E != 0` | `w[3:1]` per-channel overflow (bit3=ch3, bit2=ch2, bit1=ch1) |
| TS-MSB (ch0) | top bits 0, `w & 0x020 != 0`, `w[7:6]==0` | `w[4]`=ts_en, `w[0]`=timestamp counter MSB |
| SOP | top bits 0, `w[7:6] != 0`, `w & 0x020 == 0` | `w[7:6]`=channel 1..3, `w[4]`=ts_en, `w[3:0]`=seqnum |
| EOP | top bits 0, `w[7:6] != 0`, `w & 0x020 != 0` | `w[7:6]`=channel, `w[4:0]`=CRC-5 |
| DATA | `w[9:8] != 0` | `w[9:8]`=channel 1..3, `w[7:0]`=payload byte |

Note the channel sits in `w[7:6]` for SOP/EOP (their top bits are zero) but in `w[9:8]` for
DATA. Route each word to the owning channel's state machine accordingly.

### 3.3 Per-channel packet state machine

Three independent state machines (channels 1..3); their words interleave on the wire. Per packet:

```
SOP(channel, ts_en, seq)
  -> if ts_en: DATA (timestamp delta high byte), DATA (low byte)
  -> DATA (dbgid, 3..255)
  -> DATA x par_cnt: byte pairs, high byte first, each pair = one 16-bit param word
  -> EOP(channel, crc5)      // par_cnt = number of 16-bit words collected, up to 4
```

- dbgid 0..2 are reserved (0 debug, 1 timestamp, 2 sim-end): handle or skip, never dispatch as logs.
- Param width comes from the dbgid (section 4). A 32-bit arg consumes two 16-bit words, low half
  first: `arg = params[i] | (params[i+1] << 16)`. A 16-bit arg is one word.
- seqnum wraps at 16 and is used to detect dropped packets per channel.

### 3.4 CRC-5

- 5-bit CRC, poly 0x05, init 0x1F, **MSB-first**. Accumulate `word[7:0]` for SOP, timestamp,
  header, and data words, then the top 3 bits (`word[7:5]`) of EOP. The transmitted CRC is in
  `EOP[4:0]`. A good packet's received CRC equals the accumulated value.
- The MSB-first convention was confirmed against a real capture: 28 of 28 real packets validate.
  The textbook reflected CRC-5/USB does *not* match the wire (it validated 1 of 28); it is kept
  only as a self-test anchor (`check("123456789") == 0x19`), never used to validate wire packets.
- A CRC failure increments a health counter and drops the packet; it never aborts decoding.

### 3.5 Timestamp reconstruction

- A 16-bit counter at 2 MHz (bit clock / 12), so 0.5 us per tick and a 32.768 ms range before
  the 16-bit counter rolls over. Each timestamped packet carries a 16-bit snapshot.
- Reconstruct the full tick count by keeping the running upper bits and merging the 16-bit
  snapshot. Rollover rule: if the merged value is more than 2000 ticks *behind* the last value,
  the counter wrapped - add 0x10000. (2000 ticks of slack tolerates out-of-order jitter without
  false rollovers.)
- Convert ticks to microseconds by dividing by 2 (0.5 us/tick); divide by 2 again for 48 MHz
  parts (`--divide-time-by-2`, e.g. CC2745, whose ticks are 0.25 us). Keep raw ticks for the
  pcap record timestamp.

## 4. Metadata: the dbgid database

The wire carries only an 8-bit dbgid per channel, not the format string. The decoder resolves
`(channel, dbgid)` to a format string, arg widths, file, and line using a text database. It
never parses an ELF itself - keeping ELF/DWARF parsing out of the hot path and out of the
shipped binary. That parsing is done once, upstream, by the SDK `elf2dbgid` tool (or, in the
Python front-end, by tilogger's own ELF reader; see section 6.2).

A database entry is one `DBG_DEF` line with 8 comma-separated fields (some quoted, and quoted
fields may themselves contain commas, so the parser splits only on top-level commas):

```
DBG_DEF(NAME, dbgid, DBGCH<ch>, argCount, "format", "file", line)
```

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| 0 | literal `DBG_DEF` (else the line is skipped) |
| 1 | symbolic name (ignored) |
| 2 | dbgid, 3..255 (0..2 reserved) |
| 3 | `DBGCH1`/`DBGCH2`/`DBGCH3` -> channel 1..3 |
| 4 | argCount: > 0 means that many 16-bit args; < 0 means that many 32-bit args; 0 means none |
| 5 | printf format string, C `%` specifiers |
| 6 | source file |
| 7 | source line |

The database is keyed on `(channel, dbgid)`. Naming convention (for parts that split logical
streams): `*_mst.h` is master-only, `*_slv.h` slave-only, `*.h` both. The current single-pin
decoder merges everything into one database; a master/slave split would only be needed if two
trace pins were probed at once.

## 5. Native decoder (`tools/rftrace-decode`, `tracedecode`)

A single Rust crate: one library plus a thin CLI binary. The hot path is allocation-light and
streaming - it never buffers a whole capture. `.sal` replay works edge-to-edge, so an 11.5
billion-sample capture is processed without ever expanding it to per-sample levels.

### 5.1 Modules

| File | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| `types.rs` | Core data model: `Word`, `WordKind`, `DecodedPacket`, `DbgDef`, `DbgIdDb`, `LogRecord`, `Health`, `DeframeCfg`. |
| `sample.rs` | Ingest: raw 1-byte-per-sample reader, and the Saleae `.sal` container reader (transition-list form; see section 8.2). |
| `deframe.rs` | Physical deframe: sample levels or an edge list -> 10-bit words. Clock recovery and framing. Shares `encode_frame`/`decode_frame` with the synth encoder. |
| `packet.rs` | Word classification and the three per-channel packet state machines; CRC check; yields `DecodedPacket` on each EOP. |
| `crc5.rs` | The incremental MSB-first CRC-5 used by both the assembler and the synth encoder, plus the reflected CRC-5/USB self-test anchor. |
| `timestamp.rs` | Running device-time reconstruction (rollover unwrap) and tick-to-microsecond conversion. |
| `dbgid.rs` | `DBG_DEF` line parser and multi-file database loader. |
| `record.rs` | `DecodedPacket` + database entry -> `LogRecord`, including the C printf engine. |
| `output.rs` | The `Output` trait and its sinks: stdout line and pcap (`DLT_USER0=147`, `||` payload). |
| `synth.rs` | The reverse path: records -> tracer words -> sample levels. The basis of the hardware-free self-tests. |
| `main.rs` | CLI: argument parsing, output wiring, batch (`run_words`) and streaming (`stream_decode`) drivers, and the `replay`/`decode`/`synth`/`tail`/`retain`/`wireshark` subcommands. |

### 5.2 Core types

```
Word(u16)                         // 10-bit word, w[9:8]=channel-id, w[7:0]=payload
WordKind = Nop | Overflow(u8) | TsMsb{ts_en,ts_msb}
         | Sop{ch,ts_en,seq} | Eop{ch,crc5} | Data{ch,byte}
DecodedPacket { channel, dbgid, seq, ts_delta: Option<u16>, params: Vec<u16>, crc_ok }
DbgDef       { dbgid, channel, arg_count: i32, fmt, file, line }
DbgIdDb      { by_key: HashMap<(u8,u8), DbgDef> }
LogRecord    { alias, channel, dbgid, seq, ts_ticks, ts_us, file, line, level, text }
Health       { framing_errors, crc_errors, overflow_words, unknown_dbgid, dropped_seq }
trait Output { on_record(&LogRecord); on_health(&Health); finish(); }
```

### 5.3 Streaming vs batch

- **Streaming (`decode`)** reads samples in 1 MiB chunks and keeps only a one-frame carry that
  holds a frame straddling a chunk boundary, so a live `sigrok-cli | tracedecode decode --raw -`
  runs endlessly in bounded memory (memory is O(chunk), not O(capture)). On an endless idle line
  the carry is capped to one frame span so a static run cannot grow memory without bound.
- **Batch (`replay`)** decodes a finite `.sal` or raw file in one pass. `.sal` input is stored
  as a transition list, so batch mode is still memory-bounded on huge captures.

`Health` counters (framing, CRC, overflow, unknown dbgid, dropped sequence) are accumulated and
printed to stderr; they never interrupt the hot loop.

## 6. Python front-end (`tools/log/tiutils/streams/rftrace`, `tilogger rftrace`)

A `tilogger` stream plugin. It is a drop-in alternative to the `itm`/`uart` transports: pick a
source and one or more tilogger outputs, and RF-tracer logs flow through the identical
presentation path.

### 6.1 Transport (`rftrace_transport.py`)

- Subclasses tilogger's `TransportABC` so the logger CLI picks it up as an input stream. Falls
  back to a plain object when tilogger is not importable, so the stdlib-only pcap adapter and its
  self-check run standalone.
- Spawns `tracedecode` with `pcap --out -`, reads the pcap `DLT_USER0` stream back, splits the
  `||` payload, and re-emits tilogger `LogPacket`s using the replay-file opcode so tilogger does
  not try to re-format text the decoder already formatted. This is exactly the `from-replayfile`
  pattern: the decoder owns decode, tilogger owns presentation.
- Sources (exactly one, enforced at the CLI): `--sal` (batch replay), `--raw` (file or stdin
  decode), `--sigrok` (live two-process pipe), `--logic2` (drive the Logic 2 Automation API).
- The `--logic2` path is batched, not streaming: the Logic Pro plus Automation API cannot stream
  mid-capture, so each window is captured to a temporary `.sal` and replayed through the same
  path. `--duration` or `--trigger` bounds a window; `--loop`/`--count` produce rolling windows.
  It polls for the Saleae device for up to 15 s after connecting, because the automation port
  starts listening a moment before the USB device enumerates; without the poll a fresh headless
  launch would see an empty device list and tear the capture down at startup.

### 6.2 ELF bridge (`elf_dbgid.py`)

`--elf <app.out>` resolves every CPU-side log straight from the application binary, with no manual
`elf2dbgid` step. It reuses tilogger's own `TraceDB` (pyelftools) to read the log sections and
emits the same `DBG_DEF` text the decoder loads as a `--dbgid` file. The wire dbgid is recovered
from the 32-bit log pointer: channel is `(pointer >> 24) & 0xF` (channel-1 pointers were relocated
so that field reads as 4, mapped back to 1, and their dbgid is `((pointer & 0xFFFF) >> 2) + 1`;
channels 2/3 are unmoved with dbgid `(pointer & 0xFFFF) >> 2`). Extra `--dbgid` files layer modem
and LRF tables (pbe/rfe/mce) on top, since those are not in the application ELF.

## 7. Glue and viewer

- **Wireshark dissector** - the existing `tilogger_dissector.lua` is reused unchanged. It splits a
  `||`-delimited text payload into eight fields (alias, device time, opcode, module, level, file,
  line, string). Because the decoder already produces that exact text, the trivial dissector works
  as-is and RF-tracer logs are indistinguishable from ITM in the UI.
- **extcap wrapper** (`extcap/rftrace-decode`) presents `tracedecode wireshark` as a discoverable,
  cross-platform capture interface. Its config exposes the `.sal` file, dbgid file(s), channel, and
  the `--divide-time-by-2` flag.
- **Shell launchers** - `scripts/sigrok-to-wireshark.sh` (standalone `sigrok-cli | tracedecode |
  wireshark` pipe, no Python) and `scripts/tilogger-rftrace.sh` (builds the binary, sets
  `$TRACEDECODE`, runs the integrated `tilogger rftrace` path).
- **Measurement tools** - `scripts/bench_decode.sh` (the dense/sparse throughput benchmark,
  section 10) and `scripts/pydecode.py` (the byte-identical Python port behind the section 12
  language decision).
- The same work made tilogger's Wireshark output run on Linux, not just Windows: the win32
  named-pipe path is guarded and a fifo path added, so `wireshark --start` auto-launches on both.

## 8. Inputs and outputs

### 8.1 Raw samples

1 byte per sample, the trace line at bit index `--channel`; from `sigrok-cli -O binary` on stdin
(live) or a file (replay).

### 8.2 Saleae `.sal` reader

A `.sal` is a ZIP holding `meta.json` plus one `digital-<N>.bin` per captured channel. The trace
pin is typically digital channel 4 (channel 5 is the cluster/slave stream). Each `digital-<N>.bin`
is Saleae's transition-list format, not raw samples, so the trace channel is a few tens of MB even
for a multi-second capture and never has to be expanded to 500 MS/s.

The `digital-<N>.bin` layout was reverse-engineered and verified against a real capture (all six
channels parse to exactly their byte length, with sample coverage matching `meta.json`):

```
header (0x33 bytes):
  "<SALEAE>" magic (8 bytes)
  u32 version = 1
  u32 = 100, u32 = 1, u8 = 0
  u64 capture start (unix ms), f64 fractional ms, u16 = 0
  u64 block count
then, per block:
  u64 begin_sample, u64 end_sample, u64 num_samples (== end - begin)
  u64 sample_rate, u64 = 1, u64 payload_len
  payload_len bytes of run lengths, each a big-endian varint:
    first byte:  bit6 = continue, bits[5:0] = high bits of the value
    later bytes: bit7 = continue, bits[6:0] appended below
    run = value + 1 samples; runs alternate level and sum to num_samples
  jump table: u32 n, u32 pad, then n entries of 20 bytes
    (u64 sample_offset, u64 run_index, u32 level); entry 0 carries the block start level
```

`meta.json` also carries the sample rate and the analyzer settings (bit rate, bits/frame, MSB,
inverted, stop, parity); the reader takes the sample rate from it and otherwise uses CLI defaults.

### 8.3 Synthetic generator

The reverse of the decode path: a list of `(channel, dbgid, args, timestamp)` becomes packets
(SOP, timestamp, header, data, EOP with a real CRC), then 12-bit frames, then oversampled sample
levels. It round-trips through the decoder to test the state machine, CRC, framing, and printf
without any hardware, and it is what the large-scale performance captures (section 11) are built
from. The `synth` subcommand emits one packet per dbgid def (up to 16, stable order), with
per-channel seqnums and a monotonically advancing shared timestamp, so a decode of its output
must report zero seq gaps and monotonic time; `--idle-frames K` prepends K NOP frames per
pattern and `--repeat N` tiles the pattern N times, streamed to disk, which is how the multi-GB
dense (K small) and sparse (K large) benchmark captures are built.

### 8.4 Output sinks

- **stdout** - one human line per record, matching tilogger's default `alias | time | module |
  level | file:line | text`.
- **pcap / Wireshark** - classic pcap, magic `0xA1B2C3D4`, snaplen 256, linktype `DLT_USER0 = 147`,
  one packet per record, flushed per record so live viewers see each log immediately. Payload is
  UTF-8, `||`-delimited, byte-identical to tilogger: `alias || device_time(.9f) || opcode ||
  module || level || file || line || text`. For trace records: opcode is `LOG_OPCODE_FORMATED_TEXT`,
  module is `DBGCH<channel>`, level defaults to `INFO` (dbgids carry no level).
- **extcap** - the same pcap written to Wireshark's fifo.
- **retain** - the pcap piped into `dumpcap -b files:N -b filesize:X` for a last-N ring on disk.
- **tail** - an in-memory ring of the last N records, printed on finish; no Wireshark.

## 9. CLI surface

### 9.1 `tracedecode`

```
tracedecode <subcommand> [options] [outputs]

subcommands (one input):
  replay <file.sal>        decode a Saleae capture (batch)
  decode --raw <file|->     decode raw samples, streaming, endless
  synth  --dbgid <f> --out  fabricate a raw capture from a dbgid database (self-test);
                            --repeat <N> tiles the pattern, --idle-frames <K> pads NOP frames
  tail   <file.sal>         decode and keep the last N records
  retain <file.sal>         decode into a dumpcap ring
  wireshark ...             extcap protocol (queries or --capture --fifo)

physical / deframe options:
  --channel <n>            LA channel/bit index of the trace pin (default 4)
  --samplerate <Hz>        sample rate (default 500e6; taken from .sal meta on replay)
  --baud <Hz>              tracer bit rate (default 24e6)
  --invert / --no-invert   line inversion hint (polarity is auto-detected regardless)
  --divide-time-by-2       48 MHz tracer parts (0.25 us ticks), read time as wall-clock

metadata / labelling:
  --dbgid <path>           a DBG_DEF file; repeatable
  --alias <name>           device alias in the log (default rftrc)

outputs (one or more): stdout | pcap --out <file|-> | wireshark
retain: --files <N> --filesize <KB>
tail:   --last <N>
extcap: --extcap-interfaces | --extcap-dlts | --extcap-config | --capture --fifo <path>
```

### 9.2 `tilogger rftrace`

Sources (exactly one): `--sal`, `--raw`, `--sigrok "<args>"`, `--logic2`.
Metadata (at least one): `--elf <app.out>`, `--dbgid <file>` (repeatable).
Shared: `--channel`, `--samplerate`, `--baud`, `--divide-time-by-2`, `--alias`, `--tracedecode`.
`--logic2` knobs: `--duration`, `--trigger {rising|falling|pulse-high|pulse-low}`,
`--trigger-channel`, `--after`, `--buffer-mb`, `--loop`, `--count`, `--threshold`,
`--logic2-port`, `--logic2-address`, `--logic2-device`.
Outputs are tilogger's own (`stdout`, `wireshark`, `to-replayfile`, ...), chained tilogger-style.

## 10. Testing

Most of the weight sits at module level on synthetic data, then integration, then the CLI
surface, then gated externals. Run everything with `cargo test` (Rust) and
`python -m pytest tests/` from `tools/log/tiutils/streams/rftrace` (Python, tiutils venv).

**Rust unit tests (in each `src/` module, ~50 tests):** word/SOP/EOP layout helpers; CRC-5
(wire form vs bitwise reference, USB anchor, the two conventions must disagree); deframe
(frame codec round-trip both bit orders, idle-only, empty/static input, polarity auto-detect
on both line conventions, corrupt stop bit counts a framing error, truncated final frame left
for the streaming carry); packet state machines (classify mask table, stray DATA/EOP ignored,
corrupt byte fails CRC, per-channel seq-gap counting, seq wrap without loss, interleaved
channels, SOP mid-packet restart, overflow words); timestamp (seed, rollover, jitter slack,
hold); dbgid parser (embedded commas, reserved-id rejection, C escapes, later files override,
missing file errors); printf (specifiers, flags/width/precision, degenerate inputs, UTF-8
format strings pass through intact); record resolve (known/unknown dbgid, 16/32-bit arg
reconstruction, timestamp hold); `.sal` reader (varint runs, block-boundary edge, bad
magic/version/truncation, per-sample expansion); output (payload and stdout line pinned
byte-for-byte, pcap header shape).

**Rust integration (`tests/integration.rs`):** synth round-trips through the real deframer;
golden `.sal` end-to-end (gated on the oracle files; skips cleanly): the exact app+pbe record
sequence, monotonic timestamps, zero CRC errors; subprocess pcap contract (DLT_USER0=147 +
the eight dissector columns); streaming across 1 MiB chunk boundaries with zero loss.

**Rust CLI (`tests/cli.rs`, via the built binary):** every subcommand and flag - synth
(`--repeat`, `--idle-frames`, `--channel`); decode (`--raw` file and stdin, `--alias`,
`--channel`, `--divide-time-by-2` halves timestamps, `--samplerate`/`--baud` must match the
capture, `--invert`/`--no-invert`, `stdout` and `pcap --out` file/`-`); replay (synthetic
`.sal` built by a test-local ZIP writer, sample rate honored from meta.json); `tail --last`;
`retain --files --filesize` through real dumpcap (skips if absent); the four extcap queries
and `--capture --fifo`; every error path exits nonzero. Plus the Python-port equivalence pin:
`pydecode.py` (pure and `--numpy`) must be byte-identical to the Rust decoder on a
multi-channel synth capture; the same check against the golden `.sal` runs under
`RFTRACE_PY_GOLDEN=1` (about 35 s, so not in the default run).

**Python (`streams/rftrace/tests/`, pytest):** the pcap adapter (record parse, `||`-in-text,
header-time fallback, truncation); tracedecode resolution order (flag, env, PATH); command
construction for `--sal`/`--raw`/`--sigrok`; every `tilogger rftrace` CLI option mapped
through a recorded transport, the exactly-one-source guard, the `--logic2` duration/trigger
guards; Logic 2 capture config (timed, trigger, defaults) against the real automation
package; device polling (enumeration race, Logic Pro preference, simulation skip, deadline);
the full `--logic2` capture loop with a faked Manager (`--count`, single window, device
config, address/port); the ELF bridge (pointer-to-dbgid encoding, arg-count field, DBG_DEF
line emission, and extraction from the real reference ELF when present).

**HIL (gated, never in CI):** `RFTRACE_HIL=1 pytest tests/ -k hil` captures one live window
from a Saleae via Logic 2 and decodes it end to end (needs hardware, Logic 2 with automation,
`TRACEDECODE`, and `RFTRACE_DBGID`).

**Benchmark harness (`scripts/bench_decode.sh`):** generates a dense or sparse capture with
the synth encoder, streams it through a decode, asserts the exact record count plus all-zero
health counters, and reports MS/s and peak RSS. `DECODE` selects the decoder under test
(`SYNTH` defaults to `DECODE`), so the same harness measured the Python port:

```
DECODE=tracedecode SIZE_GB=5 scripts/bench_decode.sh dense
DECODE="python3 scripts/pydecode.py --numpy" SYNTH=tracedecode SIZE_GB=5 scripts/bench_decode.sh sparse
```

Defaults are small (0.2 GB) so it doubles as a CI-friendly stress test; point `TMPDIR` at a
real disk for 5 GB runs.

## 11. Performance (measured)

Single core, 5 GB synthetic captures from the benchmark harness (section 10), streaming
decode, correctness asserted on every run (exact record count, all health counters zero):

| Input | Throughput | vs 500 MS/s line | Peak RSS |
|---|---|---|---|
| sparse (99% NOP idle + bursts, realistic) | ~1000 MS/s | 2.0x | ~4.4 MB |
| dense (back-to-back packets, 3 channels) | ~610 MS/s | 1.2x | ~4.5 MB |

Real traffic is mostly idle NOPs, so it sits near the top row. Memory is O(chunk): a 5 GB
input decodes in under 5 MB RSS. `.sal` replay stays batch but edge-compressed - the
11.5 billion-sample golden capture decodes in about 1.2 s because it is never expanded per
sample.

## 12. Decision record: decoder language (Rust stays; measured 2026-07-04)

The decode core is Rust with a Python front-end for orchestration (tilogger integration, ELF
bridge, Logic 2 capture). Whether the whole inner decode could move to Python - collapsing the
project to one language - was settled by measurement, not preference.

**Method.** A full Python port of the hot path (`scripts/pydecode.py`: sample ingest, deframe,
packet reassembly, CRC-5, timestamp, resolve, printf) was written in two variants: pure stdlib,
and NumPy-vectorized deframe (vectorized edge extraction and batched bit-center sampling, with
an exact-scalar fallback on framing errors so output stays identical). Correctness first: both
variants are byte-identical to the Rust decoder on multi-channel synthetic captures (pinned in
`tests/cli.rs` on every test run) and on the golden 11.5 G-sample `tx_burst_example.sal`
(pure and NumPy both produce the exact 23-record output and the same health counters). Then
both were benchmarked with the section 10 harness on the 5 GB dense and sparse captures, every
run asserting exact record count and zero health counters. Single core, same machine:

| Decoder | Dense 5 GB | Sparse 5 GB | Peak RSS | Golden .sal replay |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Rust (`tracedecode`) | 608 MS/s (1.2x bar) | 1002 MS/s (2.0x) | ~4.5 MB | 1.2 s |
| Python + NumPy deframe | 194 MS/s (0.39x) | 369 MS/s (0.74x) | ~48 MB | 33 s |
| Pure Python | 55 MS/s (0.11x) | 86 MS/s (0.17x) | ~20 MB | 89 s |

**Verdict: keep Rust for the inner loop.** The bar is a live 500 MS/s stream that must decode
faster than it arrives. Pure Python misses it by roughly 6-9x; even the NumPy-vectorized
deframe misses it by 1.4x on the *best* case (sparse) and 2.6x on dense, i.e. a live capture
would fall behind on exactly the traffic a benchmark cannot promise away. The remaining Python
costs sit in the per-frame greedy accept loop and per-packet state machine, which do not
vectorize without giving up the exact framing semantics. The split stays: Rust owns
samples-to-records, Python owns orchestration.

`scripts/pydecode.py` is kept so this decision remains reproducible (and as an executable
cross-check of the wire format); it is pinned byte-identical to the Rust decoder in CI. If the
bar ever drops (slower line, batch-only decode) or PyPy/NumPy change the arithmetic, rerun:

```
DECODE="python3 scripts/pydecode.py [--numpy]" SYNTH=tracedecode SIZE_GB=5 scripts/bench_decode.sh {dense|sparse}
```

## 13. Decision log

Short records of why the architecture is shaped the way it is.

- **D1 - Off-the-shelf hardware, no FPGA.** Replace the internal capture appliance with a
  Saleae-class logic analyzer and a software deframer. Customer-scalable, no custom silicon.
- **D2 - Native decoder, not a logic-analyzer decode plugin.** A saturated line is 2 M frames/s;
  general-purpose decode plugins are single-thread Python built for kHz-to-low-MHz work. The
  decode itself is a light linear scan, so a custom native decoder easily sustains it.
- **D3 - Sampler is a subprocess.** Take samples via `sigrok-cli`'s stdout (or Logic 2 Automation
  for batched windows), keeping an arm's-length boundary from GPL-licensed capture libraries and
  a clean input abstraction. Cost is the byte-per-sample pipe overhead, acceptable at these rates.
- **D4 - Drop NOP idle at the decoder, before pcap.** The idle line spews NOP words at the frame
  rate; dropping them (and consuming timestamp-MSB and overflow words as state) collapses capture
  size to real log volume. Only complete, CRC-valid packets become pcap records.
- **D5 - Clock recovery from start edges; auto-detect polarity.** The periodic active-high start
  pulses (one per frame, continuous during idle) give a phase reference; the minority-level rule
  removes the need to hard-code inversion. Robust over long captures and across probe conventions.
- **D6 - Packetize at the log-record level.** One pcap packet per decoded log, not per frame, so
  the packet rate tracks real log volume and Wireshark stays responsive.
- **D7 - Retention via `dumpcap` ring.** No custom persistence; stock tooling gives last-N on disk.
- **D8 - `DLT_USER0=147` and reuse the existing Lua dissector.** The SDK already defines this
  Wireshark contract (link type, one packet per log, a `||`-text payload). Emitting the same text
  gives instant field/column/filter parity and zero new dissector work, at the cost of tying the
  emitter to the `||` text schema.
- **D9 - Metadata via dbgid files, single source of truth.** The decoder consumes `DBG_DEF` files
  (from `elf2dbgid`, or from the Python ELF bridge) and never parses an ELF. Decode stays correct
  across firmware rebuilds with no ELF/DWARF parsing in the hot binary.
- **D10 - Ship pre-decoded text, not binary.** Full host-side decode plus `||` text reuses the
  trivial dissector and matches tilogger exactly. Larger packets than a binary record, but the
  decode cost is off the device and the payoff is one uniform log view across all sinks.
- **D11 - HW timestamp as record time.** Use the tracer's embedded timestamp (rollover-unwrapped),
  not `sample_index / samplerate`, so times track the device clock. Sample index is a fallback only.
- **D12 - Standalone backend, adapted into tilogger.** The decoder is a standalone native backend
  whose output is byte-identical to the ITM/UART path; the `tilogger rftrace` transport is a thin
  adapter on top, not a rewrite. Best throughput and portability, one uniform log view.
- **D13 - Extcap for the production Wireshark path.** A discoverable, cross-platform capture
  interface, replacing the Windows-only named pipe of the original tilogger Wireshark output; a
  fifo path remains for quick prototyping.
- **D14 - Decoder stays Rust; measured, not argued.** A byte-identical Python port (pure and
  NumPy) misses the 500 MS/s live bar on 5 GB dense and sparse benchmarks (best case 369 MS/s
  vs Rust's 1002 MS/s). Numbers and method in section 12; the port is kept as a reproducible
  cross-check.

## 14. Risks

| Risk | Impact | Mitigation |
|---|---|---|
| Wrong inversion / bit order on a new capture | garbage deframe | polarity auto-detected; NOP=0x000 and CRC-5 pass are the self-check; `--no-invert`/`--baud` overrides exist |
| Sustained 500 MS/s capture drops samples | no realtime capture | validate the sampler driver early; batched Logic 2 windows as a fallback |
| Pipe bound at 500 MB/s (byte per sample) | sample loss / stall | switch to a packed sample feed if the pipe becomes the bottleneck |
| Wireshark chokes on a burst | dropped display | packetize at log level; the ring buffer absorbs |
| dbgid space (256 per channel) exhausted | ambiguous IDs | measure usage on real images; document the ceiling |
| Metadata drifts vs firmware | wrong decode | rebuild the dbgid table per firmware build (or use `--elf`) |
| Clock drift over long runs | framing errors | phase resync on every start edge |
